  "response": "# Plan: removing hardcoded local paths\n\n## Scope\n\nThe scan reports **exactly one** `hardcoded_local_path` finding out of 39 in total. That one finding is the whole sweep.\n\n| ID | File | Line | Literal path (from evidence) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00016` | `src/api/files.ts` | 9 | `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 `` |\n\n## crime_00016: `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json`\n\n**Current code** (`src/api/files.ts:9`):\n```ts\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\";\n```\n`loadPlanSchema()` passes this path to `existsSync`, `statSync` and `readFileSync`, and returns `null` when the file is missing.\n\n**What I checked:** `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` doesn't exist on this machine either. So `loadPlanSchema()` already returns `null` everywhere, even on the machine whose path is hardcoded. The `existsSync` → `return null` guard hides this, so the path isn't only unportable: the code is silently broken right now.\n\n**Recommended anchor: an env var, with a fallback relative to the repo root.** Don't use `os.homedir()`.\n\n- **Why not `os.homedir()`:** The scanner suggests `os.homedir()`, but here it's the wrong fit. `path.join(os.homedir(), \"dev/crimes/schema.json\")` only works if every developer, CI runner and deploy host keeps a `crimes` checkout at `~/dev/`. That still assumes one person's folder layout. A plan schema is an application asset, not per-user data such as a config dotfile or cache, so the home directory isn't its natural location.\n- **Why an env var:** The schema lives outside this app's repo (the repo root is `.../crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`, and the schema path points into a different tree). A file owned by another project should have its location supplied by whoever deploys this app.\n- **Why the repo-root fallback:** If the schema should ship with this app, copy it into the repo (for example `schemas/plan.schema.json`) and resolve it relative to the module, so a fresh clone works with no setup.\n\n**Proposed replacement:**\n```ts\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\nimport path from \"node:path\";\n\nconst REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), \"../..\");\n\nconst SCHEMA_PATH =\n  process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH ??\n  path.join(REPO_ROOT, \"schemas\", \"plan.schema.json\");\n```\n(If the package compiles to CommonJS, use `__dirname` instead of `import.meta.url`.)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Copy the schema into `schemas/plan.schema.json` from wherever it really lives now. If no one can find it, that's worth knowing, since the code has been running without it.\n2. Replace line 9 with the code above.\n3. Document `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` in `.env.example`.\n4. Decide what should happen when the file is missing. A configured path that doesn't exist should probably throw or log rather than `return null`. Otherwise the next bad path will fail as quietly as this one.\n5. Add a test that sets `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` to a fixture file and checks that `loadPlanSchema()` returns parsed JSON rather than `null`.\n\n## Related, but outside this sweep\n\n- **`crime_00015`** sits one line above, at `src/api/files.ts:8`: `` `localhost:3000` `` in `const ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\"`. It's a `hardcoded_localhost` finding, not `hardcoded_local_path`, so it isn't in scope. It's the same kind of portability bug in the same file, though, and fits in the same PR with the same fix: `process.env.ADMIN_URL`, with the localhost value kept in `.env.example`.\n- The file also uses synchronous filesystem calls inside what its header comment calls a request handler. The scan doesn't flag that and it isn't a portability problem, but it's worth knowing about while you're editing this file.\n\n## Checking the result\n\nRe-run `crimes scan --format json` and confirm that no findings of type `hardcoded_local_path` remain, and `crime_00015` too if you include it. Then search `src/` for `/Users/`, `/home/` and `C:\\\\Users\\\\` to catch any path the detector might have skipped, such as one built by joining strings.",
